{"ann_models": "ANN models refer to Artificial Neural Network models used in the context of predicting outcomes in liver transplantation, specifically the probability of graft survival and the probability of graft loss. These models are designed to analyze complex data and provide predictions based on the input variables related to donor-recipient pairs.|Here is another description: ANN models, or Artificial Neural Network models, are a type of machine learning model inspired by the human brain. They are used for complex pattern recognition and prediction tasks, and in this context, they showed better performance in predicting graft survival compared to LR-based models."},
{"ann_models__superiority__compared_with_conventional_statistical_metrics_and_published_scores": "The term \"superiority\" in this context refers to the comparative advantage or better performance of ANN models over other methods. It suggests that ANN models are more effective or provide more accurate predictions when compared to conventional statistical metrics and published scores."},
