{"uni_or_multi_objective": "Uni- or multi-objective refers to the capability of algorithms to be configured to pursue either a single goal or multiple goals simultaneously. In the context of organ transplantation, this means that algorithms can be set to optimize one specific aspect of donor-recipient matching or to balance several factors at once."},
{"anns": "Artificial Neural Networks (ANNs) are a type of AI model that mimics the functional architecture of neurons, with input and output layers. They are used in various applications, including decision-making processes in organ transplantation.|Here is another description: Artificial Neural Networks (ANNs) are a type of AI model that mimics the functional architecture of neurons, with input and output layers. They are designed to handle large databases and can be used for various classification tasks, including donor-recipient matching in organ transplantation.|Here is another description: Artificial Neural Networks (ANNs) are a type of AI model that mimics the functional architecture of neurons, with input and output layers. They are used in various applications, including organ transplantation, to handle a multitude of variables in donor-recipient matching.|Here is another description: Artificial Neural Networks (ANNs) are a type of AI model that mimics the functional architecture of neurons, with input and output layers. They are designed to handle large databases and improve generalizability with more data.|Here is another description: ANNs, or artificial neural networks, are a type of AI model that mimics the functional architecture of neurons. They are used in donor-recipient matching to process large databases and improve decision-making. However, ANNs are sensitive to the quality of data and operate as black-box models where the exact weight of each variable is not discernible."},
