{"a_rule_based_system_capable_of_establishing_when_a_survival_probability_difference_in_the_ann_ccr_model_and_a_loss_probability_difference_with_the_ann_ms_model_are_relevant": "A rule-based system capable of establishing when a survival probability difference in the ANN-CCR model and a loss probability difference with the ANN-MS model are relevant is a system that uses predefined rules to assess whether the differences in probabilities calculated by the ANN-CCR and ANN-MS models are significant enough to influence clinical decisions. This system aims to ensure that only meaningful differences in probabilities are considered, thereby improving the allocation process."},
{"rule_based_system": "A rule-based system is a method of decision-making that uses a set of predefined rules to determine the best course of action. In the context of the provided text, the rule-based system is designed to address issues related to the prediction of graft survival and loss in liver transplantation by establishing when differences in survival and loss probabilities are clinically relevant."},
{"rule_based_system__must_resolve__possible_ties_between_d_r_pairs": "The phrase \"must resolve\" indicates a requirement or necessity for the rule-based system to handle or make decisions about specific situations. In this context, it refers to the system's role in determining how to deal with instances where there are no clear differences in survival or loss probabilities between donor-recipient (D-R) pairs, ensuring fair and clinically relevant outcomes."},
{"possible_ties_between_d_r_pairs": "Possible ties between D-R pairs refer to situations where the survival and loss probabilities predicted by the ANN-CCR and ANN-MS models, respectively, are very close or identical for different donor-recipient combinations. These ties need to be resolved to ensure that the allocation of organs is both fair and clinically meaningful, particularly in scenarios where the differences in probabilities are not significant enough to make a clear decision."},
{"ann_models": "ANN models refer to Artificial Neural Network models used in the context of predicting outcomes in liver transplantation, specifically the probability of graft survival and recipient/graft losses. These models are designed to maximize the probability of a donor-recipient (D-R) pair belonging to either the \"surviving\" or \"non-surviving\" class."},
